      "content": "That sounds a bit too cautious for this meeting. If we mention a \"technical review\" or a \"transition plan\" for 4(c), Vance is going to smell blood in the water and immediately start digging into the specifics of why we're hesitating. He'll see it as a weakness in the bill.\n\nWe don't have time for a review. We need him to sign off on the bill *now*. \n\nIs there any way we can frame 4(c) not as a risk, but as a necessary stabilizer? For example, can we argue that the specific structure of the credit ensures the subsidies go toward the most efficient, high-performing manufacturers first, thereby preventing market dilution? I need you to find a way to present the current wording as a deliberate strength for the industry's long-term health, rather than a flaw.",
